
# Module Structure & Naming Conventions (Kani)

## Module Folder Structure

For every module, use the following folder layout:

- **index.ts** at module root — public exports
- **types/** — types and interfaces; one file per feature (group related types in one file to avoid many small files)
  - **No nested interfaces:** Never define nested object types inline. Always extract nested objects into separate interfaces and reference them. For example, instead of `interface A { nested?: { prop: string } }`, create `interface Nested { prop: string }` and use `interface A { nested?: Nested }`.
- **enums/** — enums; one file per feature
- **classes/** — classes; one file per feature (domain classes, not GraphQL)
- **constants/** — constants; one file per feature (e.g. `binance.ts`, `bybit.ts`, `gate.ts`)
- **struct/** — struct types for blockchain-specific object structures (e.g. Sui objects, Solana accounts); one file per feature. All struct interfaces, types, and parser functions must have JSDoc comments explaining their purpose and usage.
- **graphql-types/** — **inputs/** for `@InputType`, **object-types/** for `@ObjectType` (GraphQL modules)
- **dtos/** — DTOs for REST API modules (one file per feature)

**File naming rule (non-NestJS files):**  
Name files by the **function or feature** they cover (e.g. **user.ts**, **auth.ts**, **client.ts**). Group related types in one file (e.g. all pagination types in `types/pagination.ts`). Types, enums, and classes each go in their folder with these feature-based filenames. All types, enums, and classes must have comments (JSDoc or inline as appropriate). Names like "abc", "cde" in docs are placeholders only; use real names by concern.

Example (GraphQL module):

```
user/
  index.ts
  types/user.ts
  types/auth.ts
  types/index.ts   // export * from ./user, ./auth
  enums/role.ts
  enums/status.ts
  enums/index.ts   // export * from ./role, ./status
  classes/user.ts
  classes/session.ts
  classes/index.ts // export * from ./user, ./session
  graphql-types/
    inputs/          // @InputType classes
    object-types/    // @ObjectType classes
    index.ts         // export * from ./inputs, ./object-types
```

Example (REST API module):

```
api/
  index.ts
  types/base.ts
  types/index.ts    // export * from ./base
  classes/base.ts
  classes/index.ts  // export * from ./base
  dtos/
    rest-transform.ts
    index.ts         // export * from ./rest-transform
```

Example (CEX module with constants in subdirectories):

```
cexes/
  index.ts
  types/order-book.ts
  types/index.ts     // export * from ./order-book
  binance/
    constants.ts      // all Binance constants in one file
    last-price.service.ts
    ...
  bybit/
    constants.ts      // all Bybit constants in one file
    last-price.service.ts
    ...
  gate/
    constants.ts      // all Gate constants in one file
    last-price.service.ts
    ...
```

## NestJS File Naming (Exception)

Files that follow NestJS framework conventions must keep their original naming pattern:

- `*.service.ts`
- `*.controller.ts`
- `*.resolver.ts`
- `*.module.ts`
- `*.guard.ts`
- `*.interceptor.ts`
- `*.pipe.ts`
- `*.middleware.ts`

Example:

```
user/
  index.ts
  user.service.ts
  user.controller.ts
  user.resolver.ts
  user.module.ts
  types/user.ts
  types/auth.ts
  types/index.ts   // export * from ./user, ./auth
  enums/role.ts
  enums/status.ts
  enums/index.ts   // export * from ./role, ./status
  classes/user.ts
  classes/session.ts
  classes/index.ts // export * from ./user, ./session
```

**Rule:** Framework-level files use NestJS naming; domain-level files (types/enums/classes) are named by **function/feature** (e.g. user.ts, auth.ts) and must be commented.

**Allowed folders in module root:**  
Only these folders are allowed at module root: `types/`, `enums/`, `classes/`, `constants/`, `utils/`, `dtos/` (for REST API), `graphql-types/` (for GraphQL), and NestJS framework folders (e.g. `interceptors/`, `guards/`, `pipes/`). **Remove any other folders** (e.g. `abstracts/`, etc.) and move their contents to the appropriate standard folders. **Pure functions** (stateless helpers, parsers, formatters) belong in **utils/** — one file per concern (e.g. `utils/parse-env.ts`). Constants can be placed in a `constants/` folder at module root (organized by feature, e.g. `constants/binance.ts`) or directly in subdirectories as `constants.ts` files (multiple constants in one file is allowed).

**NestJS files must not define types, enums, classes, or constants (STRICT):**
Files like `*.service.ts`, `*.controller.ts`, `*.resolver.ts`, `*.module.ts`, `*.processor.ts`, etc. must contain **ZERO** `interface` / `type` / `enum` / non-NestJS `class` / exported `const` declarations. They may only **import** them from `types/`, `enums/`, `classes/`, `constants/`, `dtos/`, or `graphql-types/`. Any inline `interface`/`type`/`enum`/`const` in a `*.service.ts` is a hard violation — extract it to the matching module-root folder (with `index.ts` barrel) and import back via `./types` / `./enums` / `./constants`.

**GraphQL schema:**  
Put `@InputType` classes in **graphql-types/inputs/** and `@ObjectType` classes in **graphql-types/object-types/** (one file per feature). Use **graphql-types/index.ts** to re-export inputs and object-types. Keep `isAbstract: true` and a clear `description` in the decorator when the type is used as a base (e.g. extended by other DTOs).

**REST API DTOs:**  
Put REST API DTO classes (e.g. classes with `@ApiProperty` decorators for Swagger) in **dtos/** (one file per feature). Use **dtos/index.ts** to re-export all DTOs. DTOs should be documented with JSDoc comments.

